{"surah": 80, "verses": [{"ayah": 1, "text": "عَبَسَ وَتَوَلَّىٰٓ ", "translation": "He [i.e., the Prophet (ﷺ) ] frowned and turned away", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "عَبَسَ", "translit": "َsَbَʿع", "root": "عبس"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "وَتَوَلَّىٰٓ", "translit": "ٰٓàَّlَwَtَw", "root": "ولي"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 2, "text": "أَن جَآءَهُ ٱلْأَعْمَىٰ ", "translation": "Because there came to him the blind man,[1] [interrupting].", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أَن", "translit": "nَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "جَآءَهُ", "translit": "ُhَءٓāَjg", "root": "جيا"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلْأَعْمَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَmْʿعَaʾْlٱ", "root": "عمي"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 3, "text": "وَمَا يُدْرِيكَ لَعَلَّهُۥ يَزَّكَّىٰٓ ", "translation": "But what would make you perceive, [O Muḥammad], that perhaps he might be purified[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يُدْرِيكَ", "translit": "َkyِrْdُy", "root": "دري"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "لَعَلَّهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَّlَʿعَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "يَزَّكَّىٰٓ", "translit": "ٰٓàَّkَّzَy", "root": "زكو"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 4, "text": "أَوْ يَذَّكَّرُ فَتَنفَعَهُ ٱلذِّكْرَىٰٓ ", "translation": "Or be reminded and the remembrance would benefit him?", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أَوْ", "translit": "ْwَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَذَّكَّرُ", "translit": "ُrَّkَّhdَy", "root": "ذكر"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "فَتَنفَعَهُ", "translit": "ُhَʿعَfnَtَf", "root": "نفع"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلذِّكْرَىٰٓ", "translit": "ٰٓàَrْkِّhdlٱ", "root": "ذكر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 5, "text": "أَمَّا مَنِ ٱسْتَغْنَىٰ ", "translation": "As for he who thinks himself without need,[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أَمَّا", "translit": "āَّmَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مَنِ", "translit": "ِnَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱسْتَغْنَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَnْغَtْsٱ", "root": "غني"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 6, "text": "فَأَنتَ لَهُۥ تَصَدَّىٰ ", "translation": "To him you give attention.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَأَنتَ", "translit": "َtnَaʾَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "لَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "تَصَدَّىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَّdَṣَt", "root": "صدي"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 7, "text": "وَمَا عَلَيْكَ أَلَّا يَزَّكَّىٰ ", "translation": "And not upon you [is any blame] if he will not be purified.[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "عَلَيْكَ", "translit": "َkْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أَلَّا", "translit": "āَّlَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "يَزَّكَّىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَّkَّzَy", "root": "زكو"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 8, "text": "وَأَمَّا مَن جَآءَكَ يَسْعَىٰ ", "translation": "But as for he who came to you striving [for knowledge]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَأَمَّا", "translit": "āَّmَaʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مَن", "translit": "nَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "جَآءَكَ", "translit": "َkَءٓāَjg", "root": "جيا"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "يَسْعَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَʿعْsَy", "root": "سعي"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 9, "text": "وَهُوَ يَخْشَىٰ ", "translation": "While he fears [Allāh],", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَهُوَ", "translit": "َwُhَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَخْشَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَhsْhkَy", "root": "خشي"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 10, "text": "فَأَنتَ عَنْهُ تَلَهَّىٰ ", "translation": "From him you are distracted.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَأَنتَ", "translit": "َtnَaʾَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "عَنْهُ", "translit": "ُhْnَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "تَلَهَّىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَّhَlَt", "root": "لهو"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 11, "text": "كَلَّآ إِنَّهَا تَذْكِرَةٌ ", "translation": "No! Indeed, they [i.e., these verses] are a reminder;", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "كَلَّآ", "translit": "ٓāَّlَk", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "إِنَّهَا", "translit": "āَhَّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "تَذْكِرَةٌ", "translit": "ٌẗَrِkْhdَt", "root": "ذكر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 12, "text": "فَمَن شَآءَ ذَكَرَهُۥ ", "translation": "So whoever wills may remember it.[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَمَن", "translit": "nَmَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "شَآءَ", "translit": "َءٓāَhs", "root": "شيا"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ذَكَرَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَrَkَhd", "root": "ذكر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 13, "text": "فِى صُحُفٍ مُّكَرَّمَةٍ ", "translation": "[It is recorded] in honored sheets,", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "صُحُفٍ", "translit": "ٍfُḥُṣ", "root": "صحف"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مُّكَرَّمَةٍ", "translit": "ٍẗَmَّrَkُّm", "root": "كرم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 14, "text": "مَّرْفُوعَةٍ مُّطَهَّرَةٍۭ ", "translation": "Exalted and purified,", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "مَّرْفُوعَةٍ", "translit": "ٍẗَʿعwُfْrَّm", "root": "رفع"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مُّطَهَّرَةٍۭ", "translit": "ٍۭẗَrَّhَṭُّm", "root": "طهر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 15, "text": "بِأَيْدِى سَفَرَةٍ ", "translation": "[Carried] by the hands of messenger-angels,", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "بِأَيْدِى", "translit": "àِdْyَaʾِb", "root": "يدي"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "سَفَرَةٍ", "translit": "ٍẗَrَfَs", "root": "سفر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 16, "text": "كِرَامٍۭ بَرَرَةٍ ", "translation": "Noble and dutiful.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "كِرَامٍۭ", "translit": "ٍۭmāَrِk", "root": "كرم"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "بَرَرَةٍ", "translit": "ٍẗَrَrَb", "root": "برر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 17, "text": "قُتِلَ ٱلْإِنسَٰنُ مَآ أَكْفَرَهُۥ ", "translation": "Destroyed [i.e., cursed] is man;[1] how disbelieving is he.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قُتِلَ", "translit": "َlِtُq", "root": "قتل"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلْإِنسَٰنُ", "translit": "ُnَٰsnِiʾْlٱ", "root": "انس"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مَآ", "translit": "ٓāَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "أَكْفَرَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَrَfْkَaʾ", "root": "كفر"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 18, "text": "مِنْ أَىِّ شَىْءٍ خَلَقَهُۥ ", "translation": "From what thing [i.e., substance] did He create him?", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "مِنْ", "translit": "ْnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَىِّ", "translit": "ِّàَaʾ", "root": "ايي"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "شَىْءٍ", "translit": "ٍءْàَhs", "root": "شيا"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "خَلَقَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَqَlَhk", "root": "خلق"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 19, "text": "مِن نُّطْفَةٍ خَلَقَهُۥ فَقَدَّرَهُۥ ", "translation": "From a sperm-drop He created him and destined for him;[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "نُّطْفَةٍ", "translit": "ٍẗَfْṭُّn", "root": "نطف"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "خَلَقَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَqَlَhk", "root": "خلق"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "فَقَدَّرَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَrَّdَqَf", "root": "قدر"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 20, "text": "ثُمَّ ٱلسَّبِيلَ يَسَّرَهُۥ ", "translation": "Then He eased the way for him;[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ثُمَّ", "translit": "َّmُht", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلسَّبِيلَ", "translit": "َlyِbَّslٱ", "root": "سبل"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "يَسَّرَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَrَّsَy", "root": "يسر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 21, "text": "ثُمَّ أَمَاتَهُۥ فَأَقْبَرَهُۥ ", "translation": "Then He causes his death and provides a grave for him.[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ثُمَّ", "translit": "َّmُht", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَمَاتَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَtāَmَaʾ", "root": "موت"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "فَأَقْبَرَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَrَbْqَaʾَf", "root": "قبر"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 22, "text": "ثُمَّ إِذَا شَآءَ أَنشَرَهُۥ ", "translation": "Then when He wills, He will resurrect him.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ثُمَّ", "translit": "َّmُht", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "إِذَا", "translit": "āَhdِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "شَآءَ", "translit": "َءٓāَhs", "root": "شيا"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "أَنشَرَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَrَhsnَaʾ", "root": "نشر"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 23, "text": "كَلَّا لَمَّا يَقْضِ مَآ أَمَرَهُۥ ", "translation": "No! He [i.e., man] has not yet accomplished what He commanded him.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "كَلَّا", "translit": "āَّlَk", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "لَمَّا", "translit": "āَّmَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "يَقْضِ", "translit": "ِḍْqَy", "root": "قضي"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مَآ", "translit": "ٓāَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "أَمَرَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَrَmَaʾ", "root": "امر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 24, "text": "فَلْيَنظُرِ ٱلْإِنسَٰنُ إِلَىٰ طَعَامِهِۦٓ ", "translation": "Then let mankind look at his food -", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَلْيَنظُرِ", "translit": "ِrُẓnَyْlَf", "root": "نظر"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلْإِنسَٰنُ", "translit": "ُnَٰsnِiʾْlٱ", "root": "انس"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "إِلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "طَعَامِهِۦٓ", "translit": "ٓۦِhِmāَʿعَṭ", "root": "طعم"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 25, "text": "أَنَّا صَبَبْنَا ٱلْمَآءَ صَبًّا ", "translation": "How We poured down water in torrents,", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أَنَّا", "translit": "āَّnَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "صَبَبْنَا", "translit": "āَnْbَbَṣ", "root": "صبب"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلْمَآءَ", "translit": "َءٓāَmْlٱ", "root": "موه"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "صَبًّا", "translit": "āًّbَṣ", "root": "صبب"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 26, "text": "ثُمَّ شَقَقْنَا ٱلْأَرْضَ شَقًّا ", "translation": "Then We broke open the earth, splitting [it with sprouts],", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ثُمَّ", "translit": "َّmُht", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "شَقَقْنَا", "translit": "āَnْqَqَhs", "root": "شقق"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلْأَرْضَ", "translit": "َḍْrَaʾْlٱ", "root": "ارض"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "شَقًّا", "translit": "āًّqَhs", "root": "شقق"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 27, "text": "فَأَنۢبَتْنَا فِيهَا حَبًّا ", "translation": "And caused to grow within it grain", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَأَنۢبَتْنَا", "translit": "āَnْtَbۢnَaʾَf", "root": "نبت"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "فِيهَا", "translit": "āَhyِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "حَبًّا", "translit": "āًّbَḥ", "root": "حبب"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 28, "text": "وَعِنَبًا وَقَضْبًا ", "translation": "And grapes and herbage", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَعِنَبًا", "translit": "āًbَnِʿعَw", "root": "عنب"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "وَقَضْبًا", "translit": "āًbْḍَqَw", "root": "قضب"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 29, "text": "وَزَيْتُونًا وَنَخْلًا ", "translation": "And olive and palm trees", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَزَيْتُونًا", "translit": "āًnwُtْyَzَw", "root": "زيت"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "وَنَخْلًا", "translit": "āًlْhkَnَw", "root": "نخل"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 30, "text": "وَحَدَآئِقَ غُلْبًا ", "translation": "And gardens of dense shrubbery", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَحَدَآئِقَ", "translit": "َqِyʾٓāَdَḥَw", "root": "حدق"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "غُلْبًا", "translit": "āًbْlُغ", "root": "غلب"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 31, "text": "وَفَٰكِهَةً وَأَبًّا ", "translation": "And fruit and grass -", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَفَٰكِهَةً", "translit": "ًẗَhِkَٰfَw", "root": "فكه"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "وَأَبًّا", "translit": "āًّbَaʾَw", "root": "ابب"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 32, "text": "مَّتَٰعًا لَّكُمْ وَلِأَنْعَٰمِكُمْ ", "translation": "[As] enjoyment [i.e., provision] for you and your grazing livestock.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "مَّتَٰعًا", "translit": "āًʿعَٰtَّm", "root": "متع"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "لَّكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَّl", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "وَلِأَنْعَٰمِكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkِmَٰʿعْnَaʾِlَw", "root": "نعم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 33, "text": "فَإِذَا جَآءَتِ ٱلصَّآخَّةُ ", "translation": "But when there comes the Deafening Blast[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَإِذَا", "translit": "āَhdِiʾَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "جَآءَتِ", "translit": "ِtَءٓāَjg", "root": "جيا"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلصَّآخَّةُ", "translit": "ُẗَّhkٓāَّṣlٱ", "root": "صخخ"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 34, "text": "يَوْمَ يَفِرُّ ٱلْمَرْءُ مِنْ أَخِيهِ ", "translation": "On the Day a man will flee from his brother", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "يَوْمَ", "translit": "َmْwَy", "root": "يوم"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَفِرُّ", "translit": "ُّrِfَy", "root": "فرر"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلْمَرْءُ", "translit": "ُءْrَmْlٱ", "root": "مرا"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مِنْ", "translit": "ْnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "أَخِيهِ", "translit": "ِhyِhkَaʾ", "root": "اخو"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 35, "text": "وَأُمِّهِۦ وَأَبِيهِ ", "translation": "And his mother and his father", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَأُمِّهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِّmُaʾَw", "root": "امم"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "وَأَبِيهِ", "translit": "ِhyِbَaʾَw", "root": "ابو"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 36, "text": "وَصَٰحِبَتِهِۦ وَبَنِيهِ ", "translation": "And his wife and his children,", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَصَٰحِبَتِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِtَbِḥَٰṣَw", "root": "صحب"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "وَبَنِيهِ", "translit": "ِhyِnَbَw", "root": "بني"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 37, "text": "لِكُلِّ ٱمْرِئٍ مِّنْهُمْ يَوْمَئِذٍ شَأْنٌ يُغْنِيهِ ", "translation": "For every man, that Day, will be a matter adequate for him.[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "لِكُلِّ", "translit": "ِّlُkِl", "root": "كلل"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱمْرِئٍ", "translit": "ٍyʾِrْmٱ", "root": "مرا"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مِّنْهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhْnِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "يَوْمَئِذٍ", "translit": "ٍhdِyʾَmْwَy", "root": "يوم"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "شَأْنٌ", "translit": "ٌnْaʾَhs", "root": "شان"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "يُغْنِيهِ", "translit": "ِhyِnْغُy", "root": "غني"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 38, "text": "وُجُوهٌ يَوْمَئِذٍ مُّسْفِرَةٌ ", "translation": "[Some] faces, that Day, will be bright -", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وُجُوهٌ", "translit": "ٌhwُjgُw", "root": "وجه"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَوْمَئِذٍ", "translit": "ٍhdِyʾَmْwَy", "root": "يوم"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مُّسْفِرَةٌ", "translit": "ٌẗَrِfْsُّm", "root": "سفر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 39, "text": "ضَاحِكَةٌ مُّسْتَبْشِرَةٌ ", "translation": "Laughing, rejoicing at good news.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ضَاحِكَةٌ", "translit": "ٌẗَkِḥāَḍ", "root": "ضحك"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مُّسْتَبْشِرَةٌ", "translit": "ٌẗَrِhsْbَtْsُّm", "root": "بشر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 40, "text": "وَوُجُوهٌ يَوْمَئِذٍ عَلَيْهَا غَبَرَةٌ ", "translation": "And [other] faces, that Day, will have upon them dust.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَوُجُوهٌ", "translit": "ٌhwُjgُwَw", "root": "وجه"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَوْمَئِذٍ", "translit": "ٍhdِyʾَmْwَy", "root": "يوم"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "عَلَيْهَا", "translit": "āَhْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "غَبَرَةٌ", "translit": "ٌẗَrَbَغ", "root": "غبر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 41, "text": "تَرْهَقُهَا قَتَرَةٌ ", "translation": "Blackness will cover them.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "تَرْهَقُهَا", "translit": "āَhُqَhْrَt", "root": "رهق"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "قَتَرَةٌ", "translit": "ٌẗَrَtَq", "root": "قتر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 42, "text": "أُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ هُمُ ٱلْكَفَرَةُ ٱلْفَجَرَةُ ", "translation": "Those are the disbelievers, the wicked ones.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ", "translit": "َkِyʾَٰٓl۟wُaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "هُمُ", "translit": "ُmُh", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلْكَفَرَةُ", "translit": "ُẗَrَfَkْlٱ", "root": "كفر"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلْفَجَرَةُ", "translit": "ُẗَrَjgَfْlٱ", "root": "فجر"}], "hasIhya": false}], "tafsir": {"1": [{"arabic": "قريش فشق ذلك على النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم فأنزل الله تعالى عبس وتولى أن جاءه الأعمى وما يدريك لعله يزكى أو يذكر فتنفعه الذكرى يعني ابن مكتوم أما من استغنى فأنت له تصدى (4) يعني هذا الشريف وعن النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم أنه قال يؤتى بالعبد يوم القيامة فيعتذر الله تعالى إليه كما يعتذر الرجل للرجل في الدنيا فيقول وعزتي وجلالي ما زويت الدنيا عنك لهوانك علي ولكن لما أعددت لك من الكرامة والفضيلة اخرج يا عبدي إلى هذه الصفوف فمن أطعمك في أو كساك في يريد بذلك وجهي فخذ بيده فهو لك والناس يومئذ قد ألجمهم العرق فيتخلل الصفوف وينظر من فعل ذلك به فيأخذ بيده ويدخله الجنة // حديث يؤتى بال", "english": "The Messiah, peace be upon him, said: \"With difficulty shall the rich man enter Paradise.\"", "book_title": "Poverty and Abstinence", "section_title_ar": "Section 1071", "section_title_en": "The Virtue of Poverty and the Condemnation of Wealth — Section 1071", "section_index": 1071, "anchors": "- Root: دخل (dakhala)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of entering, penetrating, or intruding into a space or state.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Often used for entering into a condition or abode, including the afterlife states of Paradise and Hell.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for physical entry; metaphorical for entering into a spiritual state or reckoning.\n- Root: فقر (faqara)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To hollow out, pierce, or be in a state of neediness; the poor one is \"hollowed\" of worldly goods.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Denotes the state of one lacking sufficient means; in the Kalam context, it signifies a spiritual station of utter dependence upon God, not merely material lack.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for material destitution; metaphorical for the soul's essential neediness before its Lord.\n- Root: غني (ghaniya)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To be rich, self-sufficient, or free from need.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In theological terms, absolute Ghina (Self-Sufficiency) belongs to God alone; human wealth is a relative accident (ʿaraḍ) that often breeds heedlessness.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for possessing abundance; metaphorical for the false sense of independence that veils one from God.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Prepositional Interposition (باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور) — Governing the syntactic flow of conditional and circumstantial clauses, ensuring the periodic structure of the narrative remains unbroken across multiple embedded quotations.", "content_type": "afterlife", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on the journey of the soul, death, and the Hereafter, from 'Poverty and Abstinence'", "badge": "Afterlife"}], "8": [{"arabic": "عبد وهما في العبودية سيان ولكن في الحال مختلفان فقال وأما من جاءك يسعى وهو يخشى فأنت عنه تلهى وقال فى الآخرة أما من استغنى فأنت له تصدى وكذلك أمره بالقعود مع طائفة فقال عز وجل وإذا جاءك الذين يؤمنون بآياتنا فقل سلام عليكم وأمره بالإعراض عن غيرهم فقال وإذا رأيت الذين يخوضون في آياتنا فأعرض عنهم حتى قال فلا تقعد بعد الذكرى مع القوم الظالمبن وقال تعالى واصبر نفسك مع الذين يدعون ربهم بالغداة والعشي فكذلك الانبساط والإدلال يحتمل من بعض العباد دون بعض فمن انبساط الأنس قول موسى عليه السلام إن هي إلا فتنتك تضل بها من تشاء وتهدي من تشاء وقوله في التع", "english": "As for Adam (peace be upon him), it was said concerning him: {«وعصى آدم ربه فغوى»} \"And Adam disobeyed his Lord, so he went astray\" (Q. 20:121), then {«اجتباه ربه فتاب عليه وهدى»} \"his Lord chose him, and turned to him in forgiveness, and guided him\" (Q. 20:122).", "book_title": "Love, Longing, Intimacy and Contentment", "section_title_ar": "Section 1187", "section_title_en": "On the Varieties of Divine Proximity (Al-Inbisāṭ) and Awe (Al-Haybah) in the States of the Prophets and Saints", "section_index": 1187, "anchors": "- Root: بسط (B-S-T)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning is \"to spread out, extend, expand\" — both physically (basting a garment) and metaphorically.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: \"بسط الشيء: نشره وتوسيعه\" — used for expansion of mercy, favor, and the soul's comfort. In Kalam, it contrasts with *qabḍ* (contraction/withholding).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): \"بسط الثوب والفراش إذا نشره.\" Metaphorical (Majaz): \"بسطني ما بسطك\" (what pleases you pleases me) — hence *inbisāṭ* here denotes the soul's expansive intimacy with God, a spiritual state, not a physical act.\n- Root: أبلس (B-L-S)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"أبلس: انقطعت حجته ويئس\" — to be reduced to silence, to despair.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الإبلاس: الحزن المعترض من شدة اليأس\" — the despair of Iblis is a theological state of being cut off from mercy.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: despair/silence. Metaphorical: Iblis (the proper name) is derived from this root — he is the one \"despairing\" of divine mercy.\n- Root: عله (not used; avoided per instruction) — *Note: The text's key term \"الإدلال\" (al-idlāl) is from root دلو (D-L-W).*\n- Root: دلو (D-L-W)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"دلا الدلو: أخرجها من البئر\" — to draw water from a well; hence *iddalā'a* (إدلال) means to advance boldly, relying on a familiar bond.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Not a primary Quranic term, but lexically implies confident approach based on prior intimacy.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: lowering the bucket. Metaphorical: \"أدلّ عليه\" — to act with bold familiarity toward someone who loves you, trusting in their affection.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — Applied in the phrase \"فإنما هي عند ذوي الاعتبار من الأسماء\" (they are but names to those who reflect), restricting the ontological status of stories to mere signifiers for the discerning.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Love, Longing, Intimacy and Contentment'", "badge": "Insight"}], "17": [{"arabic": "الله فإن في القرآن علم الأولين والآخرين لمن فتحت بصيرته وقد قال تعالى قتل الإنسان ما أكفره من أي شيء خلقه من نطفة خلقه فقدره ثم السبيل يسره ثم أماته فأقبره ثم إذا شاء أنشره فقد أشارت الآية إلى أول خلق الإنسان وإلى آخر أمره وإلى وسطه فلينظر الإنسان ذلك ليفهم معنى هذه الآية أما أول الإنسان فهو أنه لم يكن شيئا مذكورا وقد كان في حيز العدم دهورا بل لم يكن لعدمه أول وأي شيء أخس وأقل من المحو والعدم وقد كان كذلك في القدم ثم خلقه الله من أرذل الأشياء ثم من أقذرها إذ قد خلقه من تراب ثم من نطفة ثم من علقة ثم من مضغة ثم جعله عظما ثم", "english": "As for his knowledge of his Lord, His greatness, and His glory — the discourse thereon is lengthy, and it is the ultimate end of the science of unveiling (ʿilm al-mukāshafah). As for his knowledge of himself, that too is lengthy; but we shall mention therefrom that which benefits in arousing humility and lowliness. And it suffices him to understand the meaning of a single verse in the Book of God — for in the Qurʾān is the knowledge of the first and the last, for him whose inner sight has been opened. God, may He be exalted, has said: {«قُتِلَ الْإِنْسَانُ مَا أَكْفَرَهُ ۝ مِنْ أَيِّ شَيْءٍ خَلَقَهُ ۝ مِنْ نُطْفَةٍ خَلَقَهُ فَقَدَّرَهُ ۝ ثُمَّ السَّبِيلَ يَسَّرَهُ ۝ ثُمَّ أَمَاتَهُ فَأَقْبَرَهُ ۝ ثُمَّ إِذَا شَاءَ أَنْشَرَهُ»} [ʿAbasa: 17–22] — \"May man be killed! How ungrateful he is! From what thing did He create him? From a drop of fluid He created him and proportioned him. Then the path He made easy for him. Then He causes him to die and buries him. Then when He wills, He raises him.\" The verse has pointed to the first origin of man, to the last of his affair, and to his middle state. Let man, then, look upon that, in order to understand the meaning of this verse.", "book_title": "Contemplation", "section_title_ar": "Section 863", "section_title_en": "Exposition of the Character Traits of the Humble and the Sum Total of Wherein the Effect of Humility and Arrogance Appears — and the Exposition of the Method of Treating Arrogance and Acquiring Humility", "section_index": 863, "anchors": "- Root: تواضع (tawāḍuʿ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Root و-ض-ع: to place down, lower, set in a low position; al-tawāḍuʿ is self-lowering, the voluntary assumption of lowness.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-waḍʿ is placing a thing in a station; tawāḍuʿ is the soul's self-abasement before truth and before the Creator, opposed to takabbur (self-elevation).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Haqīqah: physical lowering of position. Majāz: the soul's humility and abandonment of claims to eminence.\n- Root: كبر (kibr)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Root ك-ب-ر: bigness, magnitude; al-kibriyāʾ is exclusive divine majesty; al-kibr (arrogance) is the creature's false claim to that majesty.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-kibr is the soul's self-magnification, a claim to a station of greatness that belongs to God alone; it is the root of all disobedience.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Haqīqah: physical greatness. Majāz: the heart's disdain of others and refusal of truth — the blameworthy kibr.\n- Root: نطف (nuṭfah)\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-nuṭfah: pure water, used for the seminal fluid. {«ثُمَّ جَعَلْنَاهُ نُطْفَةً فِي قَرَارٍ مَكِينٍ»} [Al-Muʾminūn: 13].\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Haqīqah: clear water, little or much. Majāz: \"laylah naṭūf\" — a night raining until morning.\n- Root: خلق (khalaqa)\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-khalq: measured estimation (taqdīr mustaqīm); used for bringing forth from nothing without model, and for producing a thing from another thing.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Haqīqah: the tailor's cutting cloth to measure. Majāz: God's origination of creatures upon a measure necessitated by wisdom.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Bāb al-ʿaṭf ʿalā al-ḍamīr al-marfūʿ al-muttaṣil (Coordination upon the connected nominative pronoun) — permissible when preceded by an explicit subject, as in \"خلقه من نطفة فقدره\" where the sequence of verbs coordinates without repetition of the pronoun, each verb governing its own object in ordered succession.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Contemplation'", "badge": "Insight"}], "19": [{"arabic": "من نطفة خلقك فقدرك ثم السبيل يسرك ثم أماتك فأقبرك أفتكذبينه في قوله ثم إذا شاء أنشرك فإن لم تكونى مكذبة فما لك لا تأخذين حذرك ولو أن يهوديا أخبرك في ألذ أطعمتك بأنه يضرك في مرضك لصبرت عنه وتركته وجاهدت نفسك فيه أفكان قول الأنبياء المؤيدين بالمعجزات وقول الله تعالى في كتبه المنزلة أقل عندك تأثيرا من قول يهودى يخبرك عن حدس وتخمين وظن مع نقصان عقل وقصور علم والعجب أنه لو أخبرك طفل بأن في ثوبك عقربا لرميت ثوبك في الحال من غير مطالبة له بدليل وبرهان أفكان قول الأنبياء والعلماء والحكماء وكافة", "english": "...and intelligence and acumen, while you are the most stupid and foolish of people? Do you not recognize what lies before you of the Garden and the Fire, and that you are hastening to one of the two imminently? So why do you rejoice and laugh and occupy yourself with frivolity while you are being sought for this tremendous calamity? Perhaps today you will be snatched away, or tomorrow. I see that you regard death as distant, but God regards it as near. Do you not know that everything that is coming is near, and that the distant is only that which is not coming? Do you not know that death comes suddenly, without a prior messenger, without prior appointment or covenant? It does not come in one thing rather than another, nor in winter rather than summer, nor in summer rather than winter, nor in day rather than night, nor in night rather than day. It does not come in youth rather than old age, nor in old age rather than youth. Rather, every single breath could be the moment of sudden death. And if death does not come suddenly, then illness comes suddenly, and then leads to death. So why do you not prepare for death, when it is nearer to you than anything near? Do you not ponder the saying of God Most High: {«اقترب للناس حسابهم وهم في غفلة معرضون * ما يأتيهم من ذكر من ربهم محدث إلا استمعوه وهم يلعبون * لاهية قلوبهم»} (The reckoning for mankind has drawn near, while they turn away in heedlessness. No fresh reminder comes to them from their Lord but they listen to it while they play—their hearts distracted). Woe to you, O soul! If your audacity toward disobeying God stems from your belief that God does not see you, then how immense is your disbelief! And if it is despite your knowledge of His awareness of you, then how severe is your shamelessness and how little your modesty! Woe to you, O soul! If a slave of your slaves—nay, a brother among your brothers—were to confront you with what you detest, how would your anger and hatred toward him be? So with what audacity do you expose yourself to God's hatred, His wrath, and His severe punishment? Do you think you can endure His chastisement? Far be it, far be it! Test yourself—if heedless pride has distracted you from His painful punishment, then restrain yourself for an hour in the sun, or in the bathhouse, or bring your finger near the fire, so that your capacity may become clear to you. Are you deluded by God's generosity, His bounty, and His independence from your obedience and worship? Then why do you not rely on God's generosity in the important matters of your worldly life? When an enemy approaches you, why do you devise stratagems to repel him, rather than entrusting him to God's generosity? And when you are pressed by a need for one of the worldly desires that cannot be fulfilled except through dinar and dirham, why do you strain your very soul in seeking and procuring it through various contrivances, without relying on God's generosity—so that He might lead you to a treasure, or subjugate one of His slaves to carry your need to you without any effort or seeking on your part? Do you suppose that God is generous in the Hereafter but not in this world, when you already know that the sunnah of God admits no change, and that the Lord of the Hereafter and this world is One, and that {«ليس للإنسان إلا ما سعى»} (man shall have nothing but what he strives for)?", "book_title": "Monitoring and Accounting", "section_title_ar": "Section 1251", "section_title_en": "Section 1251 — The Admonition of the Soul Concerning the Nearness of Death and the Duplicity of False Hopes", "section_index": 1251, "anchors": "- Root: قرب (q-r-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Proximity in place, time, or rank; nearness of relation or degree.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Used for spatial, temporal, and relational nearness; here, divine nearness of death and the Hour—a metaphysical proximity, not measurable distance.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) for physical closeness; metaphorical (majāz) for what is inevitable and imminent—\"كل ما هو آت قريب\" (all that is coming is near).\n- Root: موت (m-w-t)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Death as the cessation of animal life; also used metaphorically for lifelessness.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Death as transition, not annihilation; linked to resurrection (baʿth) and divine power.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for bodily death; metaphorical for the \"death\" of heedlessness or the dead heart.\n- Root: نفس (n-f-s)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The self, soul, or spirit; that which desires and is accountable.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The soul as the locus of blameworthy traits (al-nafs al-ammārah) versus the rational, discerning faculty; here, self-address in reproach.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for the animate self; metaphorical for the inner voice of conscience rebuking the lower soul.\n- Root: طلب (ṭ-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Seeking, pursuing, demanding.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The active search for something, whether physical or conceptual; here, the soul's frantic pursuit of worldly desires versus its neglect of what is truly sought from it.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for seeking provisions; metaphorical for the soul being \"sought\" by death—\"وأنت مطلوبة\" (you are being sought).\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The rhetorical separation between the preposition and its object to emphasize urgency, as in \"على القرب\" and \"أقرب إليك من كل قريب,\" where proximity is stressed through syntactic placement.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Monitoring and Accounting'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "قتل الإنسان ما أكفره من أي شيء خلقه من نطفة خلقه فقدره ثم السبيل يسره ثم أماته فأقبره ثم إذا شاء أنشره فقد أشارت الآية إلى أول خلق الإنسان وإلى آخر أمره وإلى وسطه فلينظر الإنسان ذلك ليفهم معنى هذه الآية أما أول الإنسان فهو أنه لم يكن شيئا مذكورا وقد كان في حيز العدم دهورا بل لم يكن لعدمه أول وأي شيء أخس وأقل من المحو والعدم وقد كان كذلك في القدم ثم خلقه الله من أرذل الأشياء ثم من أقذرها إذ قد خلقه من تراب ثم من نطفة ثم من علقة ثم من مضغة ثم جعله عظما ثم كسا العظم لحما فقد كان هذا بداية وجوده حيث كان شيئا مذكورا فما صار شيئا مذكورا إلا وه", "english": "As for his knowledge of his Lord, His greatness, and His glory — the discourse thereon is lengthy, and it is the ultimate end of the science of unveiling (ʿilm al-mukāshafah). As for his knowledge of himself, that too is lengthy; but we shall mention therefrom that which benefits in arousing humility and lowliness. And it suffices him to understand the meaning of a single verse in the Book of God — for in the Qurʾān is the knowledge of the first and the last, for him whose inner sight has been opened. God, may He be exalted, has said: {«قُتِلَ الْإِنْسَانُ مَا أَكْفَرَهُ ۝ مِنْ أَيِّ شَيْءٍ خَلَقَهُ ۝ مِنْ نُطْفَةٍ خَلَقَهُ فَقَدَّرَهُ ۝ ثُمَّ السَّبِيلَ يَسَّرَهُ ۝ ثُمَّ أَمَاتَهُ فَأَقْبَرَهُ ۝ ثُمَّ إِذَا شَاءَ أَنْشَرَهُ»} [ʿAbasa: 17–22] — \"May man be killed! How ungrateful he is! From what thing did He create him? From a drop of fluid He created him and proportioned him. Then the path He made easy for him. Then He causes him to die and buries him. Then when He wills, He raises him.\" The verse has pointed to the first origin of man, to the last of his affair, and to his middle state. Let man, then, look upon that, in order to understand the meaning of this verse.", "book_title": "Contemplation", "section_title_ar": "Section 863", "section_title_en": "Exposition of the Character Traits of the Humble and the Sum Total of Wherein the Effect of Humility and Arrogance Appears — and the Exposition of the Method of Treating Arrogance and Acquiring Humility", "section_index": 863, "anchors": "- Root: تواضع (tawāḍuʿ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Root و-ض-ع: to place down, lower, set in a low position; al-tawāḍuʿ is self-lowering, the voluntary assumption of lowness.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-waḍʿ is placing a thing in a station; tawāḍuʿ is the soul's self-abasement before truth and before the Creator, opposed to takabbur (self-elevation).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Haqīqah: physical lowering of position. Majāz: the soul's humility and abandonment of claims to eminence.\n- Root: كبر (kibr)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Root ك-ب-ر: bigness, magnitude; al-kibriyāʾ is exclusive divine majesty; al-kibr (arrogance) is the creature's false claim to that majesty.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-kibr is the soul's self-magnification, a claim to a station of greatness that belongs to God alone; it is the root of all disobedience.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Haqīqah: physical greatness. Majāz: the heart's disdain of others and refusal of truth — the blameworthy kibr.\n- Root: نطف (nuṭfah)\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-nuṭfah: pure water, used for the seminal fluid. {«ثُمَّ جَعَلْنَاهُ نُطْفَةً فِي قَرَارٍ مَكِينٍ»} [Al-Muʾminūn: 13].\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Haqīqah: clear water, little or much. Majāz: \"laylah naṭūf\" — a night raining until morning.\n- Root: خلق (khalaqa)\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-khalq: measured estimation (taqdīr mustaqīm); used for bringing forth from nothing without model, and for producing a thing from another thing.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Haqīqah: the tailor's cutting cloth to measure. Majāz: God's origination of creatures upon a measure necessitated by wisdom.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Bāb al-ʿaṭf ʿalā al-ḍamīr al-marfūʿ al-muttaṣil (Coordination upon the connected nominative pronoun) — permissible when preceded by an explicit subject, as in \"خلقه من نطفة فقدره\" where the sequence of verbs coordinates without repetition of the pronoun, each verb governing its own object in ordered succession.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Contemplation'", "badge": "Insight"}], "21": [{"arabic": "قتل الإنسان ما أكفره من أي شيء خلقه من نطفة خلقه فقدره ثم السبيل يسره ثم أماته فأقبره ثم إذا شاء أنشره فقد أشارت الآية إلى أول خلق الإنسان وإلى آخر أمره وإلى وسطه فلينظر الإنسان ذلك ليفهم معنى هذه الآية أما أول الإنسان فهو أنه لم يكن شيئا مذكورا وقد كان في حيز العدم دهورا بل لم يكن لعدمه أول وأي شيء أخس وأقل من المحو والعدم وقد كان كذلك في القدم ثم خلقه الله من أرذل الأشياء ثم من أقذرها إذ قد خلقه من تراب ثم من نطفة ثم من علقة ثم من مضغة ثم جعله عظما ثم كسا العظم لحما فقد كان هذا بداية وجوده حيث كان شيئا مذكورا فما صار شيئا مذكورا إلا وهو ع", "english": "As for his knowledge of his Lord, His greatness, and His glory — the discourse thereon is lengthy, and it is the ultimate end of the science of unveiling (ʿilm al-mukāshafah). As for his knowledge of himself, that too is lengthy; but we shall mention therefrom that which benefits in arousing humility and lowliness. And it suffices him to understand the meaning of a single verse in the Book of God — for in the Qurʾān is the knowledge of the first and the last, for him whose inner sight has been opened. God, may He be exalted, has said: {«قُتِلَ الْإِنْسَانُ مَا أَكْفَرَهُ ۝ مِنْ أَيِّ شَيْءٍ خَلَقَهُ ۝ مِنْ نُطْفَةٍ خَلَقَهُ فَقَدَّرَهُ ۝ ثُمَّ السَّبِيلَ يَسَّرَهُ ۝ ثُمَّ أَمَاتَهُ فَأَقْبَرَهُ ۝ ثُمَّ إِذَا شَاءَ أَنْشَرَهُ»} [ʿAbasa: 17–22] — \"May man be killed! How ungrateful he is! From what thing did He create him? From a drop of fluid He created him and proportioned him. Then the path He made easy for him. Then He causes him to die and buries him. Then when He wills, He raises him.\" The verse has pointed to the first origin of man, to the last of his affair, and to his middle state. Let man, then, look upon that, in order to understand the meaning of this verse.", "book_title": "Condemnation of Pride and Conceit", "section_title_ar": "Section 863", "section_title_en": "Exposition of the Character Traits of the Humble and the Sum Total of Wherein the Effect of Humility and Arrogance Appears — and the Exposition of the Method of Treating Arrogance and Acquiring Humility", "section_index": 863, "anchors": "- Root: تواضع (tawāḍuʿ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Root و-ض-ع: to place down, lower, set in a low position; al-tawāḍuʿ is self-lowering, the voluntary assumption of lowness.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-waḍʿ is placing a thing in a station; tawāḍuʿ is the soul's self-abasement before truth and before the Creator, opposed to takabbur (self-elevation).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Haqīqah: physical lowering of position. Majāz: the soul's humility and abandonment of claims to eminence.\n- Root: كبر (kibr)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Root ك-ب-ر: bigness, magnitude; al-kibriyāʾ is exclusive divine majesty; al-kibr (arrogance) is the creature's false claim to that majesty.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-kibr is the soul's self-magnification, a claim to a station of greatness that belongs to God alone; it is the root of all disobedience.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Haqīqah: physical greatness. Majāz: the heart's disdain of others and refusal of truth — the blameworthy kibr.\n- Root: نطف (nuṭfah)\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-nuṭfah: pure water, used for the seminal fluid. {«ثُمَّ جَعَلْنَاهُ نُطْفَةً فِي قَرَارٍ مَكِينٍ»} [Al-Muʾminūn: 13].\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Haqīqah: clear water, little or much. Majāz: \"laylah naṭūf\" — a night raining until morning.\n- Root: خلق (khalaqa)\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-khalq: measured estimation (taqdīr mustaqīm); used for bringing forth from nothing without model, and for producing a thing from another thing.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Haqīqah: the tailor's cutting cloth to measure. Majāz: God's origination of creatures upon a measure necessitated by wisdom.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Bāb al-ʿaṭf ʿalā al-ḍamīr al-marfūʿ al-muttaṣil (Coordination upon the connected nominative pronoun) — permissible when preceded by an explicit subject, as in \"خلقه من نطفة فقدره\" where the sequence of verbs coordinates without repetition of the pronoun, each verb governing its own object in ordered succession.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Condemnation of Pride and Conceit'", "badge": "Insight"}], "22": [{"arabic": "ثم إذا شاء أنشرك فإن لم تكونى مكذبة فما لك لا تأخذين حذرك ولو أن يهوديا أخبرك في ألذ أطعمتك بأنه يضرك في مرضك لصبرت عنه وتركته وجاهدت نفسك فيه أفكان قول الأنبياء المؤيدين بالمعجزات وقول الله تعالى في كتبه المنزلة أقل عندك تأثيرا من قول يهودى يخبرك عن حدس وتخمين وظن مع نقصان عقل وقصور علم والعجب أنه لو أخبرك طفل بأن في ثوبك عقربا لرميت ثوبك في الحال من غير مطالبة له بدليل وبرهان أفكان قول الأنبياء والعلماء والحكماء وكافة", "english": "...and intelligence and acumen, while you are the most stupid and foolish of people? Do you not recognize what lies before you of the Garden and the Fire, and that you are hastening to one of the two imminently? So why do you rejoice and laugh and occupy yourself with frivolity while you are being sought for this tremendous calamity? Perhaps today you will be snatched away, or tomorrow. I see that you regard death as distant, but God regards it as near. Do you not know that everything that is coming is near, and that the distant is only that which is not coming? Do you not know that death comes suddenly, without a prior messenger, without prior appointment or covenant? It does not come in one thing rather than another, nor in winter rather than summer, nor in summer rather than winter, nor in day rather than night, nor in night rather than day. It does not come in youth rather than old age, nor in old age rather than youth. Rather, every single breath could be the moment of sudden death. And if death does not come suddenly, then illness comes suddenly, and then leads to death. So why do you not prepare for death, when it is nearer to you than anything near? Do you not ponder the saying of God Most High: {«اقترب للناس حسابهم وهم في غفلة معرضون * ما يأتيهم من ذكر من ربهم محدث إلا استمعوه وهم يلعبون * لاهية قلوبهم»} (The reckoning for mankind has drawn near, while they turn away in heedlessness. No fresh reminder comes to them from their Lord but they listen to it while they play—their hearts distracted). Woe to you, O soul! If your audacity toward disobeying God stems from your belief that God does not see you, then how immense is your disbelief! And if it is despite your knowledge of His awareness of you, then how severe is your shamelessness and how little your modesty! Woe to you, O soul! If a slave of your slaves—nay, a brother among your brothers—were to confront you with what you detest, how would your anger and hatred toward him be? So with what audacity do you expose yourself to God's hatred, His wrath, and His severe punishment? Do you think you can endure His chastisement? Far be it, far be it! Test yourself—if heedless pride has distracted you from His painful punishment, then restrain yourself for an hour in the sun, or in the bathhouse, or bring your finger near the fire, so that your capacity may become clear to you. Are you deluded by God's generosity, His bounty, and His independence from your obedience and worship? Then why do you not rely on God's generosity in the important matters of your worldly life? When an enemy approaches you, why do you devise stratagems to repel him, rather than entrusting him to God's generosity? And when you are pressed by a need for one of the worldly desires that cannot be fulfilled except through dinar and dirham, why do you strain your very soul in seeking and procuring it through various contrivances, without relying on God's generosity—so that He might lead you to a treasure, or subjugate one of His slaves to carry your need to you without any effort or seeking on your part? Do you suppose that God is generous in the Hereafter but not in this world, when you already know that the sunnah of God admits no change, and that the Lord of the Hereafter and this world is One, and that {«ليس للإنسان إلا ما سعى»} (man shall have nothing but what he strives for)?", "book_title": "Monitoring and Accounting", "section_title_ar": "Section 1251", "section_title_en": "Section 1251 — The Admonition of the Soul Concerning the Nearness of Death and the Duplicity of False Hopes", "section_index": 1251, "anchors": "- Root: قرب (q-r-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Proximity in place, time, or rank; nearness of relation or degree.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Used for spatial, temporal, and relational nearness; here, divine nearness of death and the Hour—a metaphysical proximity, not measurable distance.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) for physical closeness; metaphorical (majāz) for what is inevitable and imminent—\"كل ما هو آت قريب\" (all that is coming is near).\n- Root: موت (m-w-t)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Death as the cessation of animal life; also used metaphorically for lifelessness.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Death as transition, not annihilation; linked to resurrection (baʿth) and divine power.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for bodily death; metaphorical for the \"death\" of heedlessness or the dead heart.\n- Root: نفس (n-f-s)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The self, soul, or spirit; that which desires and is accountable.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The soul as the locus of blameworthy traits (al-nafs al-ammārah) versus the rational, discerning faculty; here, self-address in reproach.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for the animate self; metaphorical for the inner voice of conscience rebuking the lower soul.\n- Root: طلب (ṭ-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Seeking, pursuing, demanding.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The active search for something, whether physical or conceptual; here, the soul's frantic pursuit of worldly desires versus its neglect of what is truly sought from it.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for seeking provisions; metaphorical for the soul being \"sought\" by death—\"وأنت مطلوبة\" (you are being sought).\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The rhetorical separation between the preposition and its object to emphasize urgency, as in \"على القرب\" and \"أقرب إليك من كل قريب,\" where proximity is stressed through syntactic placement.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Monitoring and Accounting'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "أماته فأقبره ثم إذا شاء أنشره فقد أشارت الآية إلى أول خلق الإنسان وإلى آخر أمره وإلى وسطه فلينظر الإنسان ذلك ليفهم معنى هذه الآية أما أول الإنسان فهو أنه لم يكن شيئا مذكورا وقد كان في حيز العدم دهورا بل لم يكن لعدمه أول وأي شيء أخس وأقل من المحو والعدم وقد كان كذلك في القدم ثم خلقه الله من أرذل الأشياء ثم من أقذرها إذ قد خلقه من تراب ثم من نطفة ثم من علقة ثم من مضغة ثم جعله عظما ثم كسا العظم لحما فقد كان هذا بداية وجوده حيث كان شيئا مذكورا فما صار شيئا مذكورا إلا وهو", "english": "As for his knowledge of his Lord, His greatness, and His glory — the discourse thereon is lengthy, and it is the ultimate end of the science of unveiling (ʿilm al-mukāshafah). As for his knowledge of himself, that too is lengthy; but we shall mention therefrom that which benefits in arousing humility and lowliness. And it suffices him to understand the meaning of a single verse in the Book of God — for in the Qurʾān is the knowledge of the first and the last, for him whose inner sight has been opened. God, may He be exalted, has said: {«قُتِلَ الْإِنْسَانُ مَا أَكْفَرَهُ ۝ مِنْ أَيِّ شَيْءٍ خَلَقَهُ ۝ مِنْ نُطْفَةٍ خَلَقَهُ فَقَدَّرَهُ ۝ ثُمَّ السَّبِيلَ يَسَّرَهُ ۝ ثُمَّ أَمَاتَهُ فَأَقْبَرَهُ ۝ ثُمَّ إِذَا شَاءَ أَنْشَرَهُ»} [ʿAbasa: 17–22] — \"May man be killed! How ungrateful he is! From what thing did He create him? From a drop of fluid He created him and proportioned him. Then the path He made easy for him. Then He causes him to die and buries him. Then when He wills, He raises him.\" The verse has pointed to the first origin of man, to the last of his affair, and to his middle state. Let man, then, look upon that, in order to understand the meaning of this verse.", "book_title": "Condemnation of Pride and Conceit", "section_title_ar": "Section 863", "section_title_en": "Exposition of the Character Traits of the Humble and the Sum Total of Wherein the Effect of Humility and Arrogance Appears — and the Exposition of the Method of Treating Arrogance and Acquiring Humility", "section_index": 863, "anchors": "- Root: تواضع (tawāḍuʿ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Root و-ض-ع: to place down, lower, set in a low position; al-tawāḍuʿ is self-lowering, the voluntary assumption of lowness.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-waḍʿ is placing a thing in a station; tawāḍuʿ is the soul's self-abasement before truth and before the Creator, opposed to takabbur (self-elevation).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Haqīqah: physical lowering of position. Majāz: the soul's humility and abandonment of claims to eminence.\n- Root: كبر (kibr)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Root ك-ب-ر: bigness, magnitude; al-kibriyāʾ is exclusive divine majesty; al-kibr (arrogance) is the creature's false claim to that majesty.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-kibr is the soul's self-magnification, a claim to a station of greatness that belongs to God alone; it is the root of all disobedience.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Haqīqah: physical greatness. Majāz: the heart's disdain of others and refusal of truth — the blameworthy kibr.\n- Root: نطف (nuṭfah)\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-nuṭfah: pure water, used for the seminal fluid. {«ثُمَّ جَعَلْنَاهُ نُطْفَةً فِي قَرَارٍ مَكِينٍ»} [Al-Muʾminūn: 13].\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Haqīqah: clear water, little or much. Majāz: \"laylah naṭūf\" — a night raining until morning.\n- Root: خلق (khalaqa)\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-khalq: measured estimation (taqdīr mustaqīm); used for bringing forth from nothing without model, and for producing a thing from another thing.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Haqīqah: the tailor's cutting cloth to measure. Majāz: God's origination of creatures upon a measure necessitated by wisdom.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Bāb al-ʿaṭf ʿalā al-ḍamīr al-marfūʿ al-muttaṣil (Coordination upon the connected nominative pronoun) — permissible when preceded by an explicit subject, as in \"خلقه من نطفة فقدره\" where the sequence of verbs coordinates without repetition of the pronoun, each verb governing its own object in ordered succession.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Condemnation of Pride and Conceit'", "badge": "Insight"}], "24": [{"arabic": "يمتزج ماؤها بالأرض فيصير طينا وإليه الإشارة بقوله تعالى {فلينظر الإنسان إلى طعامه أنا صببنا الماء صبا ثم شققنا الأرض شقا فأنبتنا فيها حبا وعنبا وقضبا وزيتونا ونخلا} الآية ثم لا يكفي الماء والتراب إذ لو تركت في أرض ندية صلبة متراكمة لم تنبت لفقد الهواء فيحتاج إلى تركها في أرض رجوة متخلخلة يتغلغل الهواء إليها ثم الهواء لا يتحرك إليها بنفسه فيحتاج إلى ريح تحرك الهواء وتضربه بقهر وعنف على الأرض حتى ينفذ فيها وإليه الإشارة بقوله تعالى {وأرسلنا الرياح لواقح} وإنما إلقاحها في إيقاع الازدواج بين الهواء والماء والأرض ثم كل ذلك لا يغنيك لو كان في برد مفرط وشتاء شات فتحتاج إلى حرارة الربيع", "english": "Know that foods are many, and for God Most High in their creation there are many wonders beyond enumeration and successive causes without end. To mention this regarding every food would be lengthy, for foods are either medicines, or fruits, or nourishments. Let us take the nourishments, for they are the foundation, and from among them let us take a single grain of wheat, setting aside all other nourishments. We say: when a grain or grains are found, if you were to eat them, they would perish and you would remain hungry. So how necessary it is for you that the grain should grow in itself, increase, and multiply until it suffices for your complete need! Thus God Most High created in the grain of wheat powers by which it is nourished, just as He created in you. For the plant differs from you only in sensation and movement; it does not differ from you in nutrition, because it is nourished by water and draws it into its interior by means of roots, just as you are nourished and draw [food]. We shall not elaborate at length on the instruments of the plant in drawing nourishment to itself, but we shall point to its nourishment and say: just as wood and earth do not nourish you, but rather you need specific food, so too the grain is not nourished by everything, but rather needs something specific. The proof is that if you left it in the house, it would not increase, because nothing surrounds it but air, and mere air is not suitable for its nourishment. If you left it in water, it would not increase. If you left it in earth without water, it would not increase. Rather, there must be earth containing water, its water mingling with the earth until it becomes clay. To this points the saying of God Most High: {«فَلْيَنْظُرِ الْإِنْسَانُ إِلَىٰ طَعَامِهِ أَنَّا صَبَبْنَا الْمَاءَ صَبًّا ثُمَّ شَقَقْنَا الْأَرْضَ شَقًّا فَأَنْبَتْنَا فِيهَا حَبًّا وَعِنَبًا وَقَضْبًا وَزَيْتُونًا وَنَخْلًا»} — \"Then let man look at his food: how We poured down water in torrents, then cleft the earth in clefts, and caused to grow therein grain, and grapes, and green fodder, and olives, and palms\" — to the end of the verse. Then water and earth alone do not suffice, for if you left it in moist, hard, compacted earth, it would not sprout for lack of air. Thus it needs to be left in loose, crumbly earth through which air can penetrate. Then the air does not move to it by itself, so there is need for a wind that moves the air and strikes it with force and violence upon the earth until it penetrates therein. To this points His saying, Most High: {«وَأَرْسَلْنَا الرِّيَاحَ لَوَاقِحَ»} — \"And We sent the winds as fertilizers.\" Its fertilization consists only in effecting the union between air, water, and earth. Then all that would not avail you if it were in excessive cold and a wintry winter, so you need the heat of spring and summer. Thus it has become clear that its nourishment requires these four [elements]. So consider what each one requires: for water must flow to the cultivated land from seas, springs, rivers, and channels. Consider how God created the seas, caused the springs to gush forth, and made the rivers flow from them. Then the land may be elevated, and waters do not rise to it. So consider how God Most High created the clouds, and how He subjected the winds to them, to drive them by His leave to the regions of the earth — they being heavy clouds bearing water. Then consider how He sends it down in copious showers upon the lands at the time of spring and autumn, according to need. And consider how He created the mountains as reservoirs for water, from which springs burst forth gradually — for were it to issue all at once, the lands would be flooded, and the crops and livestock would perish. The graces of God in the mountains, clouds, seas, and rains cannot be enumerated. As for heat, it cannot be obtained between water and earth, for both are cold. So consider how He subjected the sun, and how He created it — despite its distance from the earth — as a heater for the earth at one time and not another, so that cold is obtained when cold is needed, and heat when heat is needed. This is one of the wisdoms of the sun, and its wisdoms are more than can be counted. Then when the plant rises above the earth, there is in the fruits a hardening and solidity, so they need a moisture to ripen them. Consider how He created the moon and made its property to be moistening, just as He made the sun's property to be heating. Thus it ripens the fruits and colors them by the ordainment of the All-Wise Fashioner. For this reason, were the trees in a shade that blocked the rising of the sun and the moon and the rest of the stars, the fruits would not ripen nor attain their colors and flavors.", "book_title": "Patience and Gratitude", "section_title_ar": "Section 1007", "section_title_en": "The Fourth Branch: On the Graces of God Most High in the Origins from Which Foods Are Produced and Become Suitable for Man to Thereafter Prepare by His Craft", "section_index": 1007, "anchors": "- Root: ن-ب-ت (n-b-t)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Emergence and growth from the earth; vegetation springing forth.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The act of causing growth attributed to God as Creator of the seed's inherent power; distinct from mere mechanical causation.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for botanical sprouting; metaphorical for the divine origination of life from dead earth.\n- Root: غ-ذ-و (gh-dh-w)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Nourishment; the intake by which a living body sustains and increases itself.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The principle of nutrition shared between animal and plant, differing only in sensory faculties and locomotion.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for feeding; used analogically to show the plant's passive reception of water via roots as man's active ingestion.\n- Root: م-ز-ج (m-z-j)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Blending and intermingling of two substances into a unified third.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The providential mixing of water with earth to form clay, a precondition for vegetative growth.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for physical mixture; here signifying the divine ordination of elemental harmony.\n- Root: ل-ق-ح (l-q-ḥ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Fertilization; impregnation of the date-palm by wind-borne pollen.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The winds as \"fertilizers\" (lawāqiḥ) — their function being to effect union between air, water, and earth.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for pollination; metaphorical in the Quranic verse for the winds' role in driving rain-bearing clouds.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — Used to isolate the essential conditions for growth (e.g., \"it is but air and mere air does not suffice\"), emphasizing exclusive causal requirements.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Patience and Gratitude'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "وقد قال الله عز وجل فلينظر الإنسان إلى طعامه قال ابن عباس إلى رجيعه وقال رجل لابن عمر إني أريد أن أسألك وأستحي قال فلا تستحي واسأل قال إذا قضى أحدنا حاجته فقام ينظر إلى ذلك منه قال نعم إن الملك يقول له انظر إلى ما بخلت به انظر إلى ماذا صار وكان بشر بن كعب يقول انطلقوا حتى أريكم الدنيا فيذهب بهم إلى مزبلة فيقول انظروا إلى ثمارهم ودجاجهم وعسلهم وسمنهم مثال آخر في نسبة الدنيا إلى الآخرة قال رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم ما الدنيا في الآخرة إلا كمثل ما يجعل أحدكم إصبعه في اليم فلينظر أحدكم بم", "english": "It resembles the phantasms of sleep and the confused dreams of slumber. The Messenger of Allah, may Allah bless him and grant him peace, said: \"The world is a dream, and its people are upon it — held to account and punished.\" {«الدنيا حلم وأهلها عليها مجازون ومعاقبون»}", "book_title": "Condemnation of the World", "section_title_ar": "Section 739", "section_title_en": "Section 739", "section_index": 739, "anchors": "*(Anchors established)*", "content_type": "ethics", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on purifying the character, moral diseases, and spiritual remedies, from 'Condemnation of the World'", "badge": "Ethics"}], "25": [{"arabic": "الدقيق الذي يقصرون عن فهمه إذ قال تعالى {أنا صببنا الماء صبا ثم شققنا الأرض شقا فأنبتنا فيها حبا وعنبا} الآية وأما الحكمة في سائر الكواكب السيارة منها والثوابت فخفية لا يطلع عليها كافة الخلق والقدر الذي يحتمله فهم الخلق أنها زينة للسماء لتستلذ العين بالنظر إليها وأشار إليه قوله تعالى {إنا زينا السماء الدنيا بزينة الكواكب} فجميع أجزاء العالم سماؤه وكواكبه ورياحه وبحاره وجباله ومعادنه ونباته وحيواناته وأعظاء حيواناته لا تخلو ذرة من ذراته عن حكم كثيرة من حكمه واحدة إلى عشرة إلى ألف إلى عشرة آلاف وكذا أعضاء الحيوان تنقسم إلا ما يعرف حكمتها كالعلم بأن العين للإبصار لا للبطش واليد للبطش لا للمشي والرجل للمشي لا للشم فأما الأعضاء الباطنة من الأمعاء والمرارة والكبد والكلية وآحاد العروق والأعصاب والعضلات وما فيها من التجاويف والالتفاف والاشتباك والانحراف والدقة والغلظ وسائر الصفات فلا يعرف الحكمة فيها سائر الناس والذين يعرفونها لا يعرفون منها إلا قدرا يسيرا بالإضافة إلى ما في علم الله تعالى {وما أوتيتم من العلم إلا قليلا} فإذن كل من استعمل شيئا في جهة غير الجهة التي خلق لها ولا على الوجه الذي أريد به فقد كفر فيه نعمة الله تعالى فمن ضرب غيره بيده فقد كفر نعمة اليد إذ خلقت له اليد ليدفع بها عن نفسه ما يهلكه ويأخذ ما ينفعه لا ليهلك بها غيره ومن نظر إلى وجه غير المحرم فقد كفر نعمة العين ونعمة الشمس إذ الإبصار يتم بهما وإنما خلقتا ليبصر بهما ما ينفعه في دينه ودنياه ويتقي بهما ما يضره فيهما فقد استعملها في غير ما اريد به وهذا لأن المراد من خلق الخلق وخلق الدنيا وأسبابها أن يستعين الخلق بهما على الوصول إلى الله تعالى ولا وصول إليه إلا بمحبته والأنس به في الدنيا والتجافي عن غرور الدنيا ولا أنس", "english": "...and there is no omnipotent one save the King, the Compeller. And when the covering is lifted from the eyes of the heedless, and they behold the matter to be thus, they will hear at that time the call of the Proclaimer: {«لِمَنِ الْمُلْكُ الْيَوْمَ لِلَّهِ الْوَاحِدِ الْقَهَّارِ»} — \"To whom belongs the sovereignty this Day? To God, the One, the Compeller\" {Q 40:16}. And indeed sovereignty belonged to God, the One, the Compeller, every single day — not exclusively on that day — but the heedless do not hear this proclamation save on that day. Thus it is tidings of what is renewed for the heedless of the unveiling of states, at a time when the unveiling will not benefit them. So we seek refuge in God, the Forbearing, the Generous, from ignorance and blindness, for it is the root cause of destruction.", "book_title": "Patience and Gratitude", "section_title_ar": "Section 982", "section_title_en": "The Unveiling of Sovereignty and the Discernment of What God Loves from What He Abhors", "section_index": 982, "anchors": "- Root: كشف (kashafa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Removal of a covering; disclosure of what was hidden.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Unveiling of the unseen realities (mukāshafah) — the lifting of the veil from the heart's eye to perceive things as they truly are.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah): removal of a physical covering. Metaphorical (majāz): the disclosure of intellectual or spiritual realities to the percipient.\n- Root: ملك (malaka)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Possession, dominion, sovereignty; the one who possesses is malik (king).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): True sovereignty (mulk) belongs exclusively to God; all created dominion is borrowed and ephemeral.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: royal authority. Metaphorical: absolute, unshared dominion — as in the Qur'anic proclamation of sole kingship on the Day of Judgment.\n- Root: شكر (shakara)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Gratitude; acknowledgment of beneficence.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Thankfulness is the recognition of the Benefactor and the employment of His blessings in that which pleases Him — not mere verbal praise.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: requital of favor. Metaphorical: the use of blessings in obedience to the Giver, which is the true meaning of gratitude.\n- Root: كفر (kafara)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Covering, concealing; ingratitude that veils the recognition of favor.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Kufr is the covering of blessings by failing to employ them in the manner intended by the Giver, or by employing them in what He abhors.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: covering a seed in the earth. Metaphorical: the concealment of God's blessings through misuse or neglect.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Restriction and Focused Predication (al-ḥaṣr wa al-taqyīd bi-innamā) — restricting the means of gratitude to two exclusive cognitive faculties, and the restriction of creation's purpose to the sole end of attaining unto God.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Patience and Gratitude'", "badge": "Insight"}], "26": [{"arabic": "الدقيق الذي يقصرون عن فهمه إذ قال تعالى {أنا صببنا الماء صبا ثم شققنا الأرض شقا فأنبتنا فيها حبا وعنبا} الآية وأما الحكمة في سائر الكواكب السيارة منها والثوابت فخفية لا يطلع عليها كافة الخلق والقدر الذي يحتمله فهم الخلق أنها زينة للسماء لتستلذ العين بالنظر إليها وأشار إليه قوله تعالى {إنا زينا السماء الدنيا بزينة الكواكب} فجميع أجزاء العالم سماؤه وكواكبه ورياحه وبحاره وجباله ومعادنه ونباته وحيواناته وأعظاء حيواناته لا تخلو ذرة من ذراته عن حكم كثيرة من حكمه واحدة إلى عشرة إلى ألف إلى عشرة آلاف وكذا أعضاء الحيوان تنقسم إلا ما يعرف حكمتها", "english": "...and there is no omnipotent one save the King, the Compeller. And when the covering is lifted from the eyes of the heedless, and they behold the matter to be thus, they will hear at that time the call of the Proclaimer: {«لِمَنِ الْمُلْكُ الْيَوْمَ لِلَّهِ الْوَاحِدِ الْقَهَّارِ»} — \"To whom belongs the sovereignty this Day? To God, the One, the Compeller\" {Q 40:16}. And indeed sovereignty belonged to God, the One, the Compeller, every single day — not exclusively on that day — but the heedless do not hear this proclamation save on that day. Thus it is tidings of what is renewed for the heedless of the unveiling of states, at a time when the unveiling will not benefit them. So we seek refuge in God, the Forbearing, the Generous, from ignorance and blindness, for it is the root cause of destruction.", "book_title": "Monotheism and Reliance", "section_title_ar": "Section 982", "section_title_en": "The Unveiling of Sovereignty and the Discernment of What God Loves from What He Abhors", "section_index": 982, "anchors": "- Root: كشف (kashafa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Removal of a covering; disclosure of what was hidden.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Unveiling of the unseen realities (mukāshafah) — the lifting of the veil from the heart's eye to perceive things as they truly are.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah): removal of a physical covering. Metaphorical (majāz): the disclosure of intellectual or spiritual realities to the percipient.\n- Root: ملك (malaka)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Possession, dominion, sovereignty; the one who possesses is malik (king).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): True sovereignty (mulk) belongs exclusively to God; all created dominion is borrowed and ephemeral.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: royal authority. Metaphorical: absolute, unshared dominion — as in the Qur'anic proclamation of sole kingship on the Day of Judgment.\n- Root: شكر (shakara)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Gratitude; acknowledgment of beneficence.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Thankfulness is the recognition of the Benefactor and the employment of His blessings in that which pleases Him — not mere verbal praise.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: requital of favor. Metaphorical: the use of blessings in obedience to the Giver, which is the true meaning of gratitude.\n- Root: كفر (kafara)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Covering, concealing; ingratitude that veils the recognition of favor.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Kufr is the covering of blessings by failing to employ them in the manner intended by the Giver, or by employing them in what He abhors.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: covering a seed in the earth. Metaphorical: the concealment of God's blessings through misuse or neglect.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Restriction and Focused Predication (al-ḥaṣr wa al-taqyīd bi-innamā) — restricting the means of gratitude to two exclusive cognitive faculties, and the restriction of creation's purpose to the sole end of attaining unto God.", "content_type": "ethics", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on purifying the character, moral diseases, and spiritual remedies, from 'Monotheism and Reliance'", "badge": "Ethics"}], "34": [{"arabic": "ويوم لا يجزى والد عن ولده ويوم يفر المرء من أخيه وأمه وأبيه ويوم لا ينطقون ولا يؤذن لهم فيعتذرون يوم لا مرد له من الله يوم هم بارزون ويوم هم على النار يفتنون يوم لا ينفع مال ولا بنون يوم لا ينفع الظالمين معذرتهم ولهم اللعنة ولهم سوء الدار يوم ترد فيه المعاذير وتبلى السرائر وتظهر الضمائر وتكشف الأستار يوم تخشع فيه الأبصار وتسكن الأصوات ويقل فيه الالتفات وتبرز الخفيات وتظهر الخطيئات يوم يساق العباد ومعهم الأشهاد ويشيب الصغير ويسكر الكبير فيومئذ وضعت الموازين ونشرت الدواوين وبرزت الجحيم وأغلى الحميم وزفرت", "english": "So prepare yourself, O wretched one, for this Day of immense magnitude, of prolonged duration, of overwhelming dominion, whose appointed time is near — a Day when you shall see the heaven split asunder, and the stars scattered in its terror, and the shining planets extinguished, and the sun folded up, and the mountains set in motion, and the pregnant camels neglected, and the wild beasts gathered together, and the seas set ablaze, and the souls paired with their bodies, and the Hellfire ignited, and Paradise brought near, and the mountains pulverized, and the earth stretched out — a Day when you shall see the earth shaken with its quaking, and the earth casts forth its burdens. On that Day shall people issue forth in scattered groups to be shown their deeds — a Day when the earth and the mountains are lifted and crushed with a single crushing. Then on that Day the Imminent Event shall come to pass, and the heaven shall split open, for on that Day it shall be frail, and the angels shall be at its edges, and eight shall bear the Throne of your Lord above them on that Day. On that Day you shall be presented — no secret of yours shall remain hidden. A Day when the mountains shall move and you shall see the earth laid bare — a Day when the earth shall be shaken with a violent shaking, and the mountains crumbled to dust, becoming scattered particles — a Day when people shall be like scattered moths, and the mountains like carded wool — a Day when every nursing mother shall forget what she nursed, and every pregnant female shall cast down her burden, and you shall see people drunk, yet they are not drunk, but the chastisement of God is severe — a Day when the earth shall be changed to other than this earth, and the heavens, and they shall appear before God, the One, the All-Compelling — a Day when the mountains shall be blown away as dust, leaving a level, bare plain, wherein you see no crookedness nor curve — a Day when you see the mountains and suppose them solid, yet they pass by like the passing of clouds — a Day when the heaven shall split open and become rosy like molten oil. Then on that Day no human nor jinn shall be asked about his sin. A Day when the disobedient shall be prevented from speech, and shall not be asked about their crimes, but shall be seized by their forelocks and their feet — a Day when every soul shall find what it did of good brought forth present, and what it did of evil, wishing that between it and that evil there were a far distance — a Day when every soul shall know what it has sent ahead, and shall witness what it has forwarded and what it has left behind — a Day when tongues shall be struck mute and the bodily members shall speak — a Day whose mention turned the hair of the Master of the Messengers white, when al-Ṣiddīq (may God be pleased with him) said to him: \"I see that you have turned white-haired, O Messenger of God.\" He said: \"Sūrat Hūd and its sisters have made me white-haired\" — namely, al-Wāqiʿah, al-Mursalāt, ʿAmma Yatasāʾalūn, and Idhā al-Shamsu Kuwwirat.", "book_title": "Remembrance of Death and Afterlife", "section_title_ar": "Section 1339", "section_title_en": "The Names and Attributes of the Day of Resurrection — An Exhortation to the Negligent", "section_index": 1339, "anchors": "- Root: يوم (yawm)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"اليوم: وقت طلوع الشمس إلى غروبها، وقد يعبر به عن مدة من الزمان أي مدة كانت\" — the day as a temporal span, extended metaphorically to any epoch or era.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"اليوم يعبر به عن وقت طلوع الشمس إلى غروبها. وقد يعبر به عن مدة من الزمان أي مدة كانت\" — theological usage denotes the appointed time of reckoning, magnified by divine attribution (أيام الله).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): a measured solar period. Metaphorical (Majaz): \"ذكر في أيام العرب كذا أي في وقائعها\" — days as epochal events; thus \"the Day\" is the supreme epochal event of divine justice.\n- Root: نفس (nafs)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: النفس: الروح والذات — the self, soul, or very essence.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"النفس: الروح... وقوله: ويحذركم الله نفسه فنفسه: ذاته\" — nafs as the innermost reality of a being; in the eschatological context, souls are \"paired\" (زوجت) with bodies for judgment.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the soul as life-principle. Metaphorical: \"دفق نفسه أي دمه\" — nafs as the total self, accountable in its entirety.\n- Root: ارض (arḍ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: الأرض: الجرم المقابل للسماء — the earth as the corporeal substratum beneath the heavens.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الأرض: الجرم المقابل للسماء... ويعبر بها عن أسفل الشيء\" — the earth as the lowest register of creation, subject to total transformation on that Day.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the physical earth. Metaphorical: \"تأرض فلان: لزم الأرض\" — to cling low; on that Day the earth itself is leveled, flattened, and replaced.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The repeated \"يوم\" clauses function as temporal restrictors (ẓurūf) binding each predicate to that single, all-encompassing moment; the accumulation of names (أسامي) serves not as mere repetition but as focused predication upon the multifarious realities of a single event.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "القيامة {يوم يفر المرء من أخيه وأمه وأبيه وصاحبته وبنيه} الآية فأي خير فيمن يفارقك في أشد أحوالك ويهرب منك وكيف تعجب به ولا ينفعك في القبر والقيامة وعلى الصراط إلا عملك وفضل الله تعالى فكيف تتكل على من لا ينفعك وتنسى نعم من يملك نفعك وضرك وموتك وحياتك السابع العجب بالمال كما قال تعالى إخبارا عن صاحب الجنتين إذ قال {أنا أكثر منك مالا وأعز نفرا} ورأى رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم رجلا غنيا جلس بجنبه فقير فانقبض عنه وجمع ثيابه فقال صلى الله عليه وسلم أخشيت أن يعدو إليك فقره (1) حديث بينما", "english": "If you say: \"He, peace and blessings be upon him, said after his words to Fāṭimah and Ṣafiyyah: 'I cannot avail you against God in anything, except that you have a tie of kinship which I will moisten with its moisture'\" — a ḥadīth [reported as]: \"Do you hope for my intercession, while the Banū ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib do not hope for it?\" — narrated by al-Ṭabarānī in al-Awsaṭ from the narration of ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar, and in it is Uṣayrim ibn Ḥawshab from Isḥāq ibn Wāṣil, and both are extremely weak — so that indicates that he will single out his relatives for intercession.", "book_title": "Condemnation of Delusion", "section_title_ar": "Section 882", "section_title_en": "Section 882 — On the Limits of Intercession, the Vices of Pride in Lineage, Numbers, Wealth, and Opinion, and Their Treatment", "section_index": 882, "anchors": "- Root: شفع (Sh-F-')\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning is pairing or joining; intercession (shafā'ah) is the joining of one's plea to another's cause.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Intercession is the seeking of benefit for another; its acceptance is contingent upon divine permission and good pleasure, as restricted by the Qur'anic qualifiers.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (haqīqah) is the act of making a number even (from watr, odd); metaphorical (majāz) is the mediator's plea before authority.\n- Root: عجب (ʿ-J-B)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Astonishment arising from an unknown cause; self-admiration (ʿujb) is a blameworthy state of being pleased with oneself.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العجب والتعجب: حالة تعرض للإنسان عند الجهل بسبب الشيء\" — a state that overtakes a person due to ignorance of the cause; hence, it is impossible regarding God.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: a wondrous thing; metaphorical: the one who performs marvels (taʿjābah). Self-admiration is the root of every error and innovation.\n- Root: رأي (R-'-Y)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Opinion, view, or judgment formed through reflection.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Ra'y is the considered opinion that may err or hit the mark; it is blameworthy when it becomes an object of ʿujb (self-admiration).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: sight/vision; metaphorical: intellectual insight. The ḥadīth \"إعجاب كل ذي رأي برأيه\" (the admiration of every man of opinion for his own view) is cited as a sign of the age's corruption.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction and Qualification) — The conditional clauses (in-sharṭ) governing intercession, e.g., {إلا لمن ارتضى} and {إلا بإذنه}, impose necessary restrictions that negate any absolute reliance on intercession.", "content_type": "afterlife", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on the journey of the soul, death, and the Hereafter, from 'Condemnation of Delusion'", "badge": "Afterlife"}]}, "wordTiming": {"minshawi": {"1": [[1, 780, 1269], [2, 1269, 3420]], "80:1": [[1, 780, 1269], [2, 1269, 3420]], "2": [[1, 470, 969], [2, 969, 3418], [3, 3418, 5450]], "80:2": [[1, 470, 969], [2, 969, 3418], [3, 3418, 5450]], "3": [[1, 20, 1172], [2, 1172, 2701], [3, 2701, 3626], [4, 3626, 6100]], "80:3": [[1, 20, 1172], [2, 1172, 2701], [3, 2701, 3626], [4, 3626, 6100]], "4": [[1, 480, 1067], [2, 1067, 2549], [3, 2549, 3837], [4, 3837, 6450]], "80:4": [[1, 480, 1067], [2, 1067, 2549], [3, 2549, 3837], [4, 3837, 6450]], "5": [[1, 360, 1869], [2, 1869, 2364], [3, 2364, 4530]], "80:5": [[1, 360, 1869], [2, 1869, 2364], [3, 2364, 4530]], "6": [[1, 410, 1688], [2, 1688, 2266], [3, 2266, 4750]], "80:6": [[1, 410, 1688], [2, 1688, 2266], [3, 2266, 4750]], "7": [[1, 720, 1864], [2, 1864, 2774], [3, 2774, 3834], [4, 3834, 5720]], "80:7": [[1, 720, 1864], [2, 1864, 2774], [3, 2774, 3834], [4, 3834, 5720]], "8": [[1, 140, 2027], [2, 2027, 2471], [3, 2471, 4891], [4, 4891, 6870]], "80:8": [[1, 140, 2027], [2, 2027, 2471], [3, 2471, 4891], [4, 4891, 6870]], "9": [[1, 20, 1050], [2, 1050, 2660]], "80:9": [[1, 20, 1050], [2, 